# Lifting State Up

We have the two halves now. State belongs in the nearest common ancestor, and callback props carry changes back up to it. Putting them together has a name: **lifting state up**.

The move is: take a state variable out of a child, put it in an ancestor, then send the value **down** as a prop and the updates **up** as a callback prop.

```text
        Parent            <- the state lives here
       /      \
   value      onChange     <- down as a prop, up as a callback
     |            |
   Child A     Child B
```

## A Controlled Input

The clearest case is a text input whose value belongs to the parent:

```{react autorun}
function ChatInput({ value, onValueChange }) {
  return (
    <input
      value={value}
      placeholder="Type a message"
      onChange={(inputEvent) => onValueChange(inputEvent.target.value)}
    />
  );
}

function Preview({ value }) {
  return <p>{value.length === 0 ? 'Enter a message' : 'Draft: ' + value}</p>;
}

function App() {
  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = React.useState('');

  return (
    <section>
      <ChatInput value={inputValue} onValueChange={setInputValue} />
      <Preview value={inputValue} />
    </section>
  );
}

renderReact(<App />);
```

Type in the box and the preview follows. Trace one keystroke:

1. The DOM fires `change` on the input.
2. `ChatInput`'s handler calls `onValueChange(inputEvent.target.value)`, sending the text **up**.
3. `App` runs `setInputValue`, so its state changes and it re-renders.
4. The new value goes **down** to both children as a prop.

`ChatInput` holds no state at all. It is handed a value and a way to report a change, which is what makes it a **controlled component**: the parent controls what it shows.

That is what makes step 4 possible. Because `Preview` reads the same state, it cannot fall out of step with the input, whatever either one does.

## Building Up: Three Interdependent Components

The pattern scales without changing. Here is a small chat, with an input, a send button, and a list of sent messages:

```{react autorun}
function ChatInput({ value, onValueChange, onSendMessage }) {
  function handleKeyDown(keyEvent) {
    if (keyEvent.key === 'Enter') {
      onSendMessage();
    }
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        value={value}
        placeholder="Type a message"
        onChange={(inputEvent) => onValueChange(inputEvent.target.value)}
        onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}
      />
      <button onClick={onSendMessage}>Send</button>
    </div>
  );
}

function ChatMessages({ messages }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {messages.map((message, index) => <li key={index}>{message}</li>)}
    </ul>
  );
}

function App() {
  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = React.useState('');
  const [messages, setMessages] = React.useState([]);

  function handleSendMessage() {
    if (inputValue.length === 0) {
      return;
    }

    setMessages([...messages, inputValue]);
    setInputValue('');
  }

  return (
    <section>
      <ChatInput
        value={inputValue}
        onValueChange={setInputValue}
        onSendMessage={handleSendMessage}
      />
      <ChatMessages messages={messages} />
    </section>
  );
}

renderReact(<App />);
```

Type something and press Enter or click Send. Both state variables live in `App`, and neither child owns anything.

Two details worth noticing.

**Sending is one callback with two triggers.** `ChatInput` calls `onSendMessage` from a click and from an Enter key. `App` does not know or care which happened, which is the point of naming the prop for the event rather than for the mechanism.

**Clearing the input is the parent's doing.** `handleSendMessage` calls `setInputValue('')`, and the input empties because its `value` prop changed. The child never clears itself, because the child does not own the text.

## When It Gets Uncomfortable

This works, and it has a limit. If the state lives five levels up from the component that needs it, every component in between has to accept props it does not use and pass them along. That is **prop drilling**, and it is what `useContext` in the next reading is for.

The rule stays the same: lift to the **nearest** common ancestor. Prop drilling is a symptom of lifting further than necessary, or of a value that genuinely belongs to the whole application, which is exactly the case context handles.

## Exercises

1. Lift the state. Right now the input owns its text, so the preview cannot see it.

   ```{react}
   function NameInput() {
     const [name, setName] = React.useState('');

     return <input aria-label="Name" value={name} onChange={(inputEvent) => setName(inputEvent.target.value)} />;
   }

   function Preview({ name }) {
     return <p>Hello, {name}</p>;
   }

   function App() {
     return (
       <section>
         <NameInput />
         <Preview name="" />
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<App />);
   ```
   ```+solution
   function NameInput({ name, onNameChange }) {
     return <input aria-label="Name" value={name} onChange={(inputEvent) => onNameChange(inputEvent.target.value)} />;
   }

   function Preview({ name }) {
     return <p>Hello, {name}</p>;
   }

   function App() {
     const [name, setName] = React.useState('');

     return (
       <section>
         <NameInput name={name} onNameChange={setName} />
         <Preview name={name} />
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<App />);
   ```
   ```+test
   // React commits its render asynchronously, so the assertions below run
   // on the next tick. Asserting immediately would find an empty container.
   setTimeout(function () {
     wrap(/function App\(\)[\s\S]*?React\.useState/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Move the `useState` call into `App`, the nearest common ancestor of the input and the preview', 'State lives in the parent');
     wrap(/function NameInput\([^)]*\)\s*\{\s*return/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('`NameInput` should hold no state of its own; it takes the value and a change callback as props', 'The input is controlled');
     wrap(/name=\{name\}/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Pass the name down to both children as a prop', 'Passed the value down');
     assert('input').should('exist').run('The input should render', 'The input renders');
     assert('p').should('have.text', 'Hello, ').run('The preview should read the same state the input writes', 'The preview reads the shared state');
   }, 50);
   ```

2. Complete the send handler so a message is added to the list and the input is cleared.

   ```{react}
   function ChatInput({ value, onValueChange, onSendMessage }) {
     return (
       <div>
         <input value={value} onChange={(inputEvent) => onValueChange(inputEvent.target.value)} />
         <button onClick={onSendMessage}>Send</button>
       </div>
     );
   }

   function ChatMessages({ messages }) {
     return <ul>{messages.map((message, index) => <li key={index}>{message}</li>)}</ul>;
   }

   function App() {
     const [inputValue, setInputValue] = React.useState('hello');
     const [messages, setMessages] = React.useState([]);

     function handleSendMessage() {
       // TODO: add inputValue to the end of messages, then clear the input
     }

     return (
       <section>
         <ChatInput value={inputValue} onValueChange={setInputValue} onSendMessage={handleSendMessage} />
         <ChatMessages messages={messages} />
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<App />);
   ```
   ```+solution
   function ChatInput({ value, onValueChange, onSendMessage }) {
     return (
       <div>
         <input value={value} onChange={(inputEvent) => onValueChange(inputEvent.target.value)} />
         <button onClick={onSendMessage}>Send</button>
       </div>
     );
   }

   function ChatMessages({ messages }) {
     return <ul>{messages.map((message, index) => <li key={index}>{message}</li>)}</ul>;
   }

   function App() {
     const [inputValue, setInputValue] = React.useState('hello');
     const [messages, setMessages] = React.useState([]);

     function handleSendMessage() {
       setMessages([...messages, inputValue]);
       setInputValue('');
     }

     return (
       <section>
         <ChatInput value={inputValue} onValueChange={setInputValue} onSendMessage={handleSendMessage} />
         <ChatMessages messages={messages} />
       </section>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<App />);
   ```
   ```+test
   setTimeout(function () {
     assert('li').should('have.length', 0).run('The list should start empty', 'The list starts empty');
     assert('button').click().run('Click Send', 'Clicked Send');
     // The state update re-renders asynchronously too, so give it a tick.
     setTimeout(function () {
       assert('li').should('have.length', 1).run('Sending should add the input`s text to the messages array', 'Added the message');
       assert('li').should('have.text', 'hello').run('The new message should be the text that was in the input', 'The message text is right');
       wrap(document.querySelector('input').value).should('equal', '').run('Clear the input afterwards by setting its state back to an empty string. The child cannot clear itself, since the parent owns the value', 'Cleared the input');
     }, 60);
   }, 50);
   ```

3. What makes an input "controlled"?

   ```{mcq}
   `ChatInput` has no `useState`. Where does the text it displays come from?
   correct: Correct! Its `value` comes down as a prop and every keystroke goes up through a callback. The parent owns the text, which is what lets other components read the same value.
   wrong: Look at what `ChatInput` receives and what it does when the input changes. It never stores anything.
   [x] From a prop, with changes reported to the parent through a callback
   [ ] From the DOM, which React reads when it needs it
   [ ] From a hidden state variable React creates for every input
   [ ] From the `placeholder` attribute
   ```

4. Who clears the input after a message is sent?

   ```{mcq}
   The box empties after Send. What actually caused that?
   correct: Correct! The parent set its own state back to an empty string, and the input's `value` prop changed as a result. The child has nothing to clear, because it never held the text.
   wrong: Ask where the text lives. Whoever owns it is the only one who can change it.
   [x] The parent set the shared state to an empty string, which flowed down as the new `value`
   [ ] The child reset its own internal state
   [ ] The browser clears an input automatically after a click
   [ ] React clears every input after a state update
   ```

